(a) Any person, company, organization or entity desiring to obtain a permit to construct and erect a tower within the unincorporated areas of the county shall file a written application with the county department of building, planning and development and pay an application fee in the amount of:
(1) One thousand dollars ($1,000.00) if the property for which the permit to construct and erect a tower is publicly owned; or
(2) Five thousand dollars ($5,000.00) if the property for which the permit to construct and erect a tower is privately owned.
(b) An application will not be considered until it is complete. The county department of building, planning and development is authorized to develop application forms to assist in providing the required information and to facilitate the application process. In addition to those requirements contained elsewhere in this article, the application shall be in compliance with the county zoning ordinance and any other applicable local ordinance and state or federal law.
(c) The following information shall be submitted when applying for any permit required by this article and must be submitted for any application to be considered complete:
The following factors shall be considered in determining whether to approve or deny the permit request:
(1) Height of proposed tower or antenna;
(2) Proximity of the tower to residential structures, schools, daycare centers, nursery schools, recreation areas, playgrounds, parks, hospitals and churches;
(3) Nature of uses of adjacent and nearby properties;
(4) Surrounding topography, tree coverage and foliage;
(5) Design of the tower, with particular reference to design characteristics that have the effect of reducing visual obtrusiveness;
(6) Proximity to other antennas or towers;
(7) Availability of suitable existing antennas or towers or other structures.
(d) In approving the permit, the county may impose additional conditions to the extent determined necessary to buffer or otherwise minimize adverse effects of the proposed tower or antenna on surrounding properties. Should the permit be denied, the decision shall be in writing, supported by substantial evidence as contained in a written record of the proceedings.
(Ord. of 10-6-98, Art. IV)

